hi many people have participated in the  daily community questions we have on our  youtube community tab and one thing was  appearing over and over and over again  well if only there was someone bored  enough to help with that to create  videos around these machine learning  quiz questions we're posting over there  hi there my name is tim and i'm  currently doing my phd on machine  learning mostly centered around  geometric and medical data  first of all please note that this video  is most likely not 100 correct perfectly  accurate or in any other way beyond  doubt or different interpretation if you  find something that you disagree with  please do argue with us in the comments  just as letizia i'm just a phd student  not someone who can even remotely claim  to know everything besides i don't think  someone like that even exists and even  the best make mistakes  and i'm far from the best  so leticia put out a question about ein  faces while an eigenface can just be  seen as a weird german english mixture  word and fun fact we call these mutant  words denglish in german it's also a way  of analyzing faces  and while eigenfaces are some more  classical computer vision invented long  before the dominance of neural networks  they are still pretty interesting the  idea is to describe a given phase  through a combination of fixed  components to be more exact a linear  combination of some bass face pieces so  to get leticia's face you could  recombine the face pieces from the  learned base to actually form her face  so how do we do that for a given input  collection of faces you represent them  as vectors then you compute the  principal component analysis of this  data basically obtaining the most  important vectors that you need to  properly express the faces in the  collection as a linear combination  so for our faces given as a set of  vectors here just shown with two  dimensions because drawing a hundred  thousand dimensions gets somewhat  cumbersome we compute the covariance  matrix then compute the first k  eigenvectors that we can then use as a  basis to approximate any input phase  vector and this is why we call these  base faces the eigenfaces because they  are phase components computed from  eigenvectors of the covariance matrix of  all the faces these eigenfaces can then  be used for things like real-time face  detection while they are still somewhat  fast and robust at least when you  compare that to other approaches that  are not deep learning based  for the next question i want to  summarize what domain adaptation is and  for clarity also what transfer learning  is in general and how that differs  while that is a bit of a fuzzy  definition we can generally say that  domain adaptation happens when one  solves the same task but changes the  kind of input the task requires for  example we train to detect dogs in an  image during the day  and after domain adaptation we still try  to detect dogs but perhaps now with  images taken during the night  we just have a different but still  related input data distribution  this domain adaptation is part of what  is called transfer learning for transfer  learning not only the domain can change  but the task itself can also change we  repurpose a model trained on one task to  be used on a different one so what  actually changes during domain  adaptation between settings  well we just swap out the data  distribution of the input and as you can  already imagine the difference whether  something is just domain adaptation or  already learning a different task is  somewhat flimsy and up for debate  where is the line in all this is  detecting dogs at night already a  different task is detecting cats so much  different that it's not just a different  data distribution but essentially a new  task  let's move on to another one which is  trying to find out how you see one of  the latest trends in machine learning  you are asked whether you believe that  causality will be the next big thing  in machine learning and roughly half of  you thought so  so what is causality in ml about and why  do we need it  well machine learning is terrible at  knowing causal relationships for example  when it is raining there are more people  outside holding umbrellas without more  information a machine learning algorithm  looking at images could also come to  think that using umbrellas actually  causes it to rain the machine learning  algorithm doesn't know how our world  works it just detects patterns and  correlations and not cause our  relationships and correlation is not  enough to establish causality  well how do humans overcome this  with trying things out does it start  pouring if you pull out your umbrella  while the sun is shining  well no it will only cause people to  stare at you weirdly but that is exactly  the problem  this trying out which is called an  intervention in causality context is  somewhat generally machine learning  doesn't do  machine learning is not embodied and  generally cannot act to generate their  own training data it just looks at data  and makes up statistical predictions  detects patterns it does not try things  out to find causal relationships so why  is that even important if we just want  to make some predictions on known data  we are fine without causality but as  soon as we want to use deeper  understanding of the domain and find  truly invariant features like by  suggesting actions we need to learn  casual structures and not just  statistical correlations we may not want  our house robot of the future to  actually have to drop our favorite vase  to find out that dropping it causes it  to break and not breaking it causes it  to drop  if you want to dive deeper into the  topic we will leave you the link to a  great talk in the description box below  and rather leave the technical parts to  the experts  and in case that was nothing new to you  what do you think let us know in the  comments whether after transformers  being one of the latest big trends now  causality is all you need i sincerely  apologize for his behavior  and lastly we have two questions that  are somewhat related first of all  letitia wanted to know what the manifold  hypothesis actually is  so a manifold for starters is any  topological space where the surroundings  of each point are similar to the  euclidean space more simply put it's a  space where for every local point we can  describe its neighborhood through a  chart which is just a flat  representation for example for a sphere  like the earth or a coffee mug if we  zoom in enough we have a flat surface  that we can create a map for we can no  longer see the curvature  so this is a manifold now the manifold  hypothesis actually states that complex  data from the real world like photos or  handwritten digits that is high  dimensional data with lots of pixels can  be compressed into a lower dimensional  form  what that means is that for these 28 by  28 pixel wide handwritten digits we can  express all plausible handwritten digits  through far less values than these 28 by  28 color values for example we could  reduce that to some information on what  handwritten digit it is and some piece  of information that describes how thick  and skewed and so on this number is in  result we'd get a representation that's  probably a lot lower dimensional than  the 784 dimensions of our pixel space  so we then have a low dimensional  representation of our data that lies in  the high dimensional image space this  lower dimensional structure this more  simple representation  that is the data manifold and what  happens to the rest of the high  dimensional space well it is populated  with data that does not make sense in  the real world in the image space this  was consists of all the images that are  pure noise in all configurations in  other words the manifold hypothesis  tells us that natural data is more  structured and constrained than one  would expect that the possibilities to  generate implausible non-existent data  points are far more numerous than  natural data which has a certain  simplicity attached to it  so to summarize the manifold hypothesis  just states that real-world data like  these handwritten digits is actually  lower dimensional than the original real  world space it lives in here the 784  pixel values and the same of course also  holds for more complex examples like  pictures of cats or representations of  geometry  a question tightly linked to the  manifold hypothesis is on what the  denoising outer encoder actually does  for denoising autoencoder you train an  autoencoder on some data that is you  feed data through a neural network that  at some point has a smaller capacity  than your input and then you try to keep  as much information alive as possible  now this should already sound somewhat  familiar to the manifold hypothesis an  autoencoder with a small bottleneck  learns a lower dimensional  representation of the input data  a noisy outer encoder then gets an input  that is actually corrupted for example  for handwritten digits we apply some  noise to the image pixels  when we then encode and decode that and  now train such that the outer encoder  does reconstruct the input this is  removing some part of the noise because  for the high dimensional input of this  image only the lower dimensional part  with actual meaning the part without the  noise the real data manifold is actually  learned and that is because it is just  way more complicated to learn random  noise patterns rather than meaningful  data and it is much more complicated to  fit that through the tiny bottleneck and  formally speaking what this actually  does for example is to learn the  manifold of plausible handwritten digits  here inside the bottleneck and for a  noisy input that's not on that manifold  the encoding process then just projects  that back onto the learned manifold  because that is all the auto encoder can  actually memorize and by then decoding  that we get a less noisy input there's  some great and easy to understand paper  in the description below with some more  thoughts about the manifold go check  that out  we hope that this was somewhat  interesting and useful to you and i'd  love to get some feedback and some  discussions below in the comments you  can also suggest new community questions  in the comments so that these become a  bit more diverse in the future thank you  very much for watching and see you in  another video hopefully thanks tim
 

